.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Daemon 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Daemon
.Nd daemonization for Perl programs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Daemon;

Fugu::Daemon->daemonize(
    logfile => '/var/log/mydaemon.log',
    pidfile => '/var/run/mydaemon.pid',
);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
turns the program into a daemon, as
.Xr daemon 3
does.
The program forks, and the parent exits.
The child becomes a session leader, redirects its standard descriptors
away from the terminal, leaves the directory it started in, and sets
its file creation mask.
.Pp
The module keeps no state that a caller can see and has one class
method.
.Ss daemonize
.Fn daemonize "%args"
forks into the background and detaches from the controlling terminal.
.Pp
The parent exits with status 0.
Thus
.Fn daemonize
returns only in the child.
The child calls
.Xr setsid 2
and reopens standard input from
.Pa /dev/null .
The child then appends standard output to
.Fa logfile
and duplicates standard output onto standard error.
It changes to the root directory and sets the file creation mask
to 022.
Last, it takes the PID file when the caller named one.
.Pp
The child redirects the descriptors before it changes the directory.
Thus a relative
.Fa logfile
keeps the meaning that the caller gave it.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "logfileXX"
.It Fa logfile
The path for standard output and standard error.
The default is
.Pa /dev/null .
This default is correct for a daemon that logs through
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p
in syslog mode.
The module opens the file in append mode.
.It Fa pidfile
The PID file the child takes with
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p .
The child acquires it after
.Xr setsid 2 ,
so the file holds the PID of the session leader.
The child holds the lock for the life of the process, even if the
caller discards the returned object.
The default is no PID file.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn daemonize
never returns in the parent.
In the child it returns the
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
object when the caller gave a
.Fa pidfile ,
and nothing otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example daemonizes the program, takes a PID file, and logs through
syslog:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $log = Fugu::Log->new(mode => 'syslog', ident => 'mydaemon');

Fugu::Daemon->daemonize(
    logfile => '/var/log/mydaemon.log',
    pidfile => '/var/run/mydaemon.pid',
);

$log->info('started, pid %d', $$);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn daemonize
dies and does not return if the fork fails, if
.Xr setsid 2
fails, if the module cannot redirect one of the three standard
descriptors, if it cannot change to the root directory, or if it cannot
take the PID file.
A daemon cannot recover from these startup conditions.
.Pp
A failure to take the PID file most often means that a second daemon
already holds it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setsid 2 ,
.Xr umask 2 ,
.Xr daemon 3 ,
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Privdrop 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Process 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
Call
.Fn daemonize
before you open files or sockets that the daemon must keep.
The child redirects the standard descriptors, but it inherits
everything that is already open with no change.
.Pp
The daemon never removes the PID file.
An
.Xr unlink 2
in a root-owned directory needs a write permission on the directory
that a daemon gives up at the privilege drop.
The
.Fn is_stale
method of
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
covers the leftover file.
